  "answer": "This has been reported before. It is a known limitation of Windows PowerShell 5.1, not of Windows Terminal. PowerShell 5.1 can't be launched with a working directory that contains `[`. It falls back to `C:\\Windows\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0`, to the home directory, or to `C:\\`. Parentheses are fine, which matches what you saw. The Terminal team says they can't work around it, and the earlier tickets were closed as duplicates of #11149. The fix that worked for earlier reporters (#14100, #19000) was to switch the profile to PowerShell 7 or later (`pwsh`). Install it, then set it as the default profile. Explorer's \"Open in Terminal\" will then start in the right folder. Command Prompt also handles these paths. One caveat: in #19595 a reporter said that with pwsh 7.5.4 as the default profile, `wt.exe` launched from a bracketed path still opened in the home directory. That is a separate issue about launching `wt.exe` with no `--startingDirectory`, not this one."
 },
